  "ai_overview": {
    "text_blocks": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "Both ACE inhibitors (angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors) and ARBs (angiotensin II receptor blockers) are equally recommended as first-line therapies for high blood pressure in major clinical guidelines .",
        "snippet_highlighted_words": [
          "Both ACE inhibitors (angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors) and ARBs (angiotensin II receptor blockers) are equally recommended as first-line therapies for high blood pressure in major clinical guidelines"
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          0,
          1
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Key Similarities"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Efficacy: Both lower blood pressure effectively by targeting the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system (RAAS) to relax and widen blood vessels.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              0,
              2,
              3
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Organ Protection: Both provide kidney and heart protective benefits, making them preferred choices for patients with diabetes, chronic kidney disease, or heart failure.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              3,
              4
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Rule: You should take either an ACE inhibitor or an ARB, never both at the same time, as combining them increases the risk of kidney dysfunction and high potassium without added benefit .",
            "reference_indexes": [
              5,
              6
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Key Differences"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Mechanism of Action: ACE inhibitors stop the body from producing angiotensin II (an enzyme blocker, ending in -pril like lisinopril). ARBs block angiotensin II from binding to its receptors (receptor blocker, ending in -sartan like losartan).",
            "reference_indexes": [
              2,
              7
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Side Effects (The Main Differentiator): ACE inhibitors frequently cause a persistent, dry, nagging cough in a notable minority of patients. ARBs do not cause this cough.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              4,
              8
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Preference Trends: Because of the lower rate of side effects (specifically the lack of cough), recent clinical analyses and studies increasingly lean toward preferentially prescribing ARBs over ACE inhibitors as an initial first-line choice.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              0,
              1
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Safety Warning: Both classes are strictly contraindicated during pregnancy due to the risk of severe fetal harm.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              3
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "If you'd like to narrow this down, let me know:"
      },
